---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Card from '../components/Card.astro';
import { PRINCIPLES } from '../data/site';
---

<Base
  title="Philosophy"
  description="Ten first principles behind Node Learn: nodes before courses, learning as structural change, capability through evidence, and a deliberate refusal of attention-farming mechanics."
>
  <Section
    eyebrow="Philosophy"
    title="Ten principles we design against."
    lede="These are constraints, not slogans. Each one rules out product decisions that would be easy, profitable or conventional — and each one is meant to be checkable against what actually ships."
    narrow
  >
    <ol class="space-y-4">
      {
        PRINCIPLES.map((p, i) => (
          <li>
            <Card id={p.id} class="scroll-mt-24">
              <div class="flex flex-wrap items-start justify-between gap-x-4 gap-y-2">
                <div class="flex items-baseline gap-3">
                  <span class="font-mono text-small font-semibold text-foreground-tertiary">
                    {String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}
                  </span>
                  <h2 class="text-h2 font-semibold text-foreground">{p.title}</h2>
                </div>
                <span class="rounded-full bg-element px-2.5 py-1 text-caption font-medium text-foreground-tertiary">
                  {p.antiGoal}
                </span>
              </div>
              <p class="mt-3 text-[17px] font-semibold leading-7 text-foreground">{p.claim}</p>
              <p class="mt-2 text-body leading-7 text-foreground-secondary">{p.detail}</p>
            </Card>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
  </Section>

  <Section
    eyebrow="Consequences"
    title="What these principles cost us."
    lede="Principles only mean something when they rule things out. Here is what we gave up."
    narrow
  >
    <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <Card>
        <h3 class="text-h3 font-semibold text-foreground">No engagement growth loop</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-small leading-6 text-foreground-secondary">
          Streaks, daily goals and push-to-return nudges reliably increase retention metrics. We do
          not use them, so our numbers will look worse than products that do.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card>
        <h3 class="text-h3 font-semibold text-foreground">No marketplace economics</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-small leading-6 text-foreground-secondary">
          Taking a cut of lesson payments is the obvious business model for a product like this. The
          MVP has no payments at all, which means no take rate and no transaction flywheel.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card>
        <h3 class="text-h3 font-semibold text-foreground">No verification badge to sell</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-small leading-6 text-foreground-secondary">
          We could run identity checks and charge teachers for a trust badge. Instead profiles are
          openly self-described, and we say so plainly wherever a profile appears.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card>
        <h3 class="text-h3 font-semibold text-foreground">No viral invitation mechanics</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-small leading-6 text-foreground-secondary">
          No contact-book uploads, no "people you may know", no referral rewards. Discovery is
          deliberately narrow: exact handles, invite links and genuine learning context.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section narrow class="pb-24">
    <div class="rounded-lg border-l-4 border-l-primary border-y border-r border-border bg-surface p-6 shadow-card">
      <h2 class="text-h3 font-semibold text-foreground">On AI</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-body leading-7 text-foreground-secondary">
        Node Learn ships no autonomous AI in the MVP. If we add assistance later it will summarise,
        structure or explain — always showing its sources and its confidence, and always correctable
        by you. A model will never be positioned as the authority on whether you have understood
        something. That judgement belongs to you and your teacher.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>
</Base>

